<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="UTF-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
  <title>marimo WebAssembly + GitHub Pages Template</title>
  <style>
    body {
      font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #333;
      background-color: #fff;
      padding: 20px;
      margin: 0;
    }
    .container {
      max-width: 1000px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }
    header {
      background-color: #f0f0f0;
      padding: 20px;
      text-align: center;
      margin-bottom: 20px;
    }
    .logo {
      width: 80px;
      height: auto;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    h1 {
      font-size: 24px;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
    }
    .subtitle {
      font-size: 16px;
      margin: 0 auto;
    }
    .section-title {
      font-size: 20px;
      margin: 20px 0 10px;
      text-align: center;
    }
    .cards {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 20px;
      margin: 20px 0;
    }
    .card {
      background-color: #f9f9f9;
      border: 1px solid #ddd;
      flex: 1 0 200px;
    }
    .card-header {
      background-color: #ddd;
      padding: 10px;
      font-weight: bold;
    }
    .card-header.app {
      background-color: #eee;
    }
    .card-body {
      padding: 15px;
    }
    .card-link {
      display: inline-block;
      background-color: #4a86e8;
      color: white;
      padding: 5px 10px;
      text-decoration: none;
    }
    .card-link.app {
      background-color: #ff9900;
    }
    footer {
      background-color: #f0f0f0;
      text-align: center;
      padding: 20px 0;
      margin-top: 30px;
    }
    .footer-links a {
      color: #4a86e8;
      margin: 0 10px;
      text-decoration: none;
    }
  </style>
</head>
<body class="font-sans max-w-2xl mx-auto p-8 leading-relaxed">    
    <header>
      <div class="container">
        <h1>Data Visualization Principles</h1>
        <p class="subtitle">What is visualizing data?</p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main class="container">
      <div>
        <h1>Be clear about what you're showing</h1>
        <p>Labels, units, filters, views</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Labels</li>
          <li>units</li>
          <li>filters</li>
          <li>Purpose</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h1>Accuracy Matters</h1>
        <p>Make sure your data are correct</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Don’t guess at ratios/charts</li>
          <li>Know your underlying data</li>
          <li>Double check your data</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
        <h1>Everything can encode data</h1>
        <p>Combinations to represent different layers of data</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Color</li>
          <li>Shape</li>
          <li>Size</li>
          <li>Motion</li>
          <li>Sound</li>
        </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>4. Visualizations should fit the data</h3>
      <p><em>Use the right visualization for the kind of data that you’re working with</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Events – Scatterplot</li>
        <li>Counts – Bar charts, histogram</li>
        <li>Proportions – Stacked bar, pie</li>
        <li>Time series – Line charts</li>
      </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>5. Keep the Context Clear</h3>
      <p><em>Always keep a sign of the data filters/manipulations that have been applied</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Filters</li>
        <li>Sorts</li>
        <li>Types</li>
      </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>6. Choose Scales Wisely</h3>
      <p><em>A scale should show the range of all possible values and relative/relevant values</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Color scales</li>
        <li>Time scales</li>
        <li>Geo-ranges</li>
      </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>7. Dashboards are not Reports</h3>
      <p><em>A report offers a snapshot, a dashboard helps a user build their own snapshot.</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Reports are read, dashboards are used</li>
        <li>The primary task for the dashboard should be immediately apparent</li>
        <li>If there are multiple tasks, filters should persist between them as much as possible</li>
      </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>8. Give the reader a headline</h3>
      <p><em>The big number is all that most users will read</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>A big number should be essential, actionable, and relevant to decisions</li>
        <li>Top and left within each component</li>
        <li>Support numbers with graphs or context</li>
      </ul>
      </div>

      <div>
      <h3>9. Scan then read</h3>
      <p><em>Prioritize the headline, lead in to the story</em></p>
      <ul>
        <li>Headlines should always be actionable</li>
        <li>Use the z-pattern</li>
        <li>Use tasks to balance density of visualizations with density of data</li>
      </ul>
      </div>
      
    </main>

    <footer>
    </footer>

</body>
</html>
